# The launcher dock and the activity bar both want the left edge, and
# both must have it — in whatever order the two commands ran.
#
# Before the side-band rule only one of them survived, and which one
# depended on the order (hunt/findings-2026-09-21/daily-dock-left-eats-activity-bar.md):
#
#   `ex dock left` then reveal (view.dock_toggle)  -> the ACTIVITY BAR
#                                                     disappeared entirely
#   reveal then `ex dock left`                     -> the DOCK disappeared
#   `ex dock always` then `ex dock left`           -> both painted, side
#                                                     by side (the shape
#                                                     the corpus asserts in
#                                                     tests/e2e/launcher_dock_move_edge.test)
#
# A side dock's band is its own three columns whether the strip is up or
# down, so the activity bar is laid out INSIDE it and a reveal fills a
# band that was already the dock's — it covers nothing. And `:dock left`
# moves a revealed strip instead of dismissing it, so the two commands
# commute.
#
# In ascii mode the activity bar is a column of section letters with `>`
# on the focused one (`>E` = Explorer); the dock's toast names the edge.
# width: 120
# height: 40
# ascii
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
# ── order one: move, then reveal ──
ex dock left
command view.dock_toggle
expect screen contains "dock"
expect screen contains ">E"
# ── order two: reveal, then move ── (back to the bottom first, so the
# move is a real one)
ex dock bottom
command view.dock_toggle
ex dock left
expect screen contains "dock"
expect screen contains ">E"
# ── order three: pin it, then move ──
ex dock always
ex dock left
expect screen contains "dock"
expect screen contains ">E"
